// A four-point flare with a hot core, one per element, each one turned to its
// own axis. The one sprite renderer here that draws a *shape* rather than a
// falloff.
//
//     cargo run -p karakuri -- examples/coil_vortex.kir examples/star_flares.kir
//
// Pairs with `coil_vortex.kir`; that file's header is where the pair's argument
// is. **Every other sprite renderer in this library is radially symmetric** —
// `soft_points` is `pow(1 - d, falloff)`, `plain_points` is that squared,
// `hard_dots` is a `smoothstep` on a mixed metric — so a sprite has no
// orientation and a field of them has no texture beyond its density. This one
// rotates `point_coord` into the element's own frame and lays a bright bar
// along each axis, which is what a lens sees looking at a point of light. At
// ten thousand elements you can see one; at a quarter of a million you could
// not, which is the whole reason the pair is written at the count it is.
//
// **The rotation is read, not computed.** `uv` carries `vec2(cos(a), sin(a))`
// for this element's axis, written once per element by `coil_vortex`, so the
// fragment block spends four multiplies and two adds where an angle would have
// cost a `hash1`, a `sin` and a `cos` **per covered pixel**. That is
// `plain_points.kir`'s argument about `tint` applied to geometry instead of to
// colour, and it is worth a fifth of this block: the same file computing its own
// angle estimates at 147 ops per fragment against this one's 122.
//
// **It consumes `size` and `uv`, and both are real constraints.** Only `age` and
// `velocity` have derivation rules, so a Set pairing this with a geometry that
// emits neither is refused where the Set is built. `hard_dots.kir` says the same
// thing about `size` and it is the honest failure: a renderer whose shape comes
// out of an attribute nobody supplies has no shape.
//
// # `blend weighted`, which was tested rather than assumed
//
// This was written additive, and both were rendered at the default capacity and
// at four times it. At 10240 the two are close — additive is slightly more
// luminous, weighted slightly more saturated. **At 40960 they are not close**:
// additive washes the dense middle of the funnel to white and the individual
// flares stop existing, which is the failure the whole pair is a reaction to,
// while weighted keeps every hue and every ribbon boundary. Since the point of a
// wide `capacity` range is that an operator turns it up, the blend that survives
// being turned up is the one to ship.
//
// What it costs is the sort. Weighted blended OIT resolves toward the nearer
// fragment by depth weight rather than by order, so the occlusion is right and
// the ordering is approximate — `glass_shell.kir` is where that is argued at
// length. Here the approximation is doing something useful rather than being
// tolerated: a ribbon in front of another ribbon *hides* it, and hiding is how a
// coil reads as a coil.
//
// **`exposure` and `opacity` are two controls and not one**, which is the other
// thing weighted buys. Under additive, doubling either does the same thing.
// Here `exposure` is how bright the flare is and `opacity` is how much of what
// is behind it survives, so a dim solid funnel and a bright transparent one are
// different pictures.
//
// # The sub-pixel floor is this file's own
//
// ADR-0245 draws a sprite whose side is below one pixel at one pixel and pays
// the lost coverage in the colour, so material can go on getting smaller without
// getting dimmer — and stops getting cheaper, at one fragment per element
// whatever the target is. A pair that fell through that floor would be a
// different picture measured against a fixed cost.
//
// `point_scale`'s range therefore starts at 0.0014, which is 1.008 pixels at 720
// rows: **no setting of this fader is sub-pixel at the reference size.** And the
// multiplier is `max(size, 1.0)` rather than `size`, so that claim is a property
// of this file rather than a claim about whichever geometry filled the slot.
// `coil_vortex` emits `size` at 1 or above by construction and the `max` never
// fires for the shipped pair; it is there for the one that has not been written
// yet. `plain_points.kir` states the same floor and cannot make it unconditional,
// because its `point_rate` is the param and nothing else.
//
// # Coverage, against the pair everything here is measured against
//
// Coverage is `capacity × (point_rate × height)²` and `point_rate` is a fraction
// of the target's height. `size` is not a constant, so the figure carries the
// mean of its square — `1 + grain·hash^5` over a uniform hash is 3.79 at the
// default `grain` of 4. At 1280x720:
//
//   this pair    10240 × (0.0076 × 720)² × 3.79  ≈ 1.16M px = 1.26x the frame
//   the default  262144 × (0.00556 × 720)²       ≈ 4.19M px = 4.55x the frame
//
// **A factor of 3.6 fewer covered pixels, and a factor of 25 fewer primitives.**
// The fragment block is dearer per pixel than `soft_points`' — 122 ops against
// 57 — so the honest figure for the fragment stage is a factor of 1.7 rather
// than of 3.6, and it is the vertex and simulation stages that fall by an order
// of magnitude. A sprite at `size` 1 is 5.5 pixels across and the largest are
// about 27; the beat pulse takes those to 34 as it passes.

proc star_flares {
  kind  L4
  blend weighted

  consumes position, tint, size, uv

  // Sprite side as a fraction of the frame's height, per unit of `size`. 0.0076
  // is 5.47 pixels at 720 rows. The floor of 0.0014 is one pixel there; see the
  // note above for why it is where it is.
  param point_scale : float [0.0014, 0.0417] = 0.0076

  // How bright the flare is, and how much of what is behind it it hides. Two
  // controls because the blend is weighted; see above.
  param exposure    : float [0.0, 8.0]  = 1.4
  param opacity     : float [0.0, 1.0]  = 0.85

  // The halo's exponent. The bars below are what make this a star; the halo is
  // what makes it a light.
  param falloff     : float [0.5, 8.0]  = 2.6

  // How much of the shape is the bars, and how thin they are. At `spike` 0 this
  // is an ordinary soft sprite, which is the far end of the same fader.
  param spike       : float [0.0, 1.0]  = 0.55
  param needle      : float [2.0, 40.0] = 16.0

  // The white core, added to the colour rather than to the coverage: a flare is
  // hotter than it is wider at the middle.
  param glint       : float [0.0, 4.0]  = 1.6

  vertex {
    clip = camera * vec4(position, 1.0);

    // `max(size, 1.0)` is the sub-pixel floor made unconditional; see above.
    point_rate = point_scale * max(size, 1.0);
  }

  fragment {
    // Into the element's own frame. `uv` is a unit vector, so this is a
    // rotation and not a scale, and it arrives flat-interpolated — a sprite is
    // one element's worth of values and there is nothing to interpolate
    // between.
    let q = point_coord * 2.0 - 1.0;
    let p = vec2(q.x * uv.x - q.y * uv.y, q.x * uv.y + q.y * uv.x);

    let d  = length(p);
    let ax = abs(p.x);
    let ay = abs(p.y);

    let halo = pow(max(0.0, 1.0 - d), falloff);

    // Two bars crossing at the middle: thin across, and fading to nothing at
    // the quad's edge so a bar never ends in a hard line.
    let bar1 = max(0.0, 1.0 - ay * needle) * max(0.0, 1.0 - ax);
    let bar2 = max(0.0, 1.0 - ax * needle) * max(0.0, 1.0 - ay);

    // `halo` to the sixth, by multiplication. It is exactly
    // `pow(max(0, 1 - d), falloff * 6)` and costs three multiplies instead of a
    // second `pow`, which is the most expensive builtin in a block that runs per
    // covered pixel.
    let h2  = halo * halo;
    let h3  = h2 * halo;
    let hot = h3 * h3;

    // Alpha is opacity here, not emission — the blend is weighted — and the
    // shader clamps it to [0, 1], so the bars' overlap at the middle resolves to
    // solid rather than to more than solid.
    color = vec4(tint * exposure + vec3(hot * glint),
                 (halo + spike * (bar1 + bar2)) * opacity);
  }
}
